  "entryNodeId": "start",
  "metadata": {
    "title": "Surds, Rationalizing Factors, and Conjugates",
    "description": "Simplify irrational roots and remove radicals from denominators with valid identities.",
    "teachingApproach": "Build the idea, derive the method, practice transfer, and use targeted remedial feedback.",
    "targetDurationMinutes": 32,
    "sourceScope": "R.S. Aggarwal, Quantitative Aptitude for Competitive Examinations, Chapter 5 “Square Roots and Cube Roots,” printed pages 180–205. Approved scope: Cover the chapter's elementary surd work: identify rational and irrational square roots, simplify roots such as √12 into a rational factor times a square-free radical, add or subtract like surds, and rationalize simple denominators. Derive conjugate products (a+b)(a−b)=a²−b² and apply them without losing signs; use the correct rationalizing factor for expressions involving sums or differences of square roots. Keep all transformations exact and do not claim a decimal approximation is exact. Learners should recognize when radicals are like terms, choose a factor that makes the denominator rational, and check the transformed expression by multiplication."
  },
  "nodes": {
    "start": {
      "id": "start",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "Surds keep irrational roots exact. Extract square factors, combine only matching radical parts, and use a conjugate when it turns a denominator into a rational number.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_1"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_1": {
      "id": "teach_1",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "√49=7 is rational, while √2 is irrational. Simplify √12 by extracting its square factor: √12=√(4×3)=2√3. A decimal approximation can help estimate, but it does not replace this exact form.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "q_1"
        }
      ]
    },
